WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.210
- Hi everyone. My name is
Ricardo Alvarez-Pimentel,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.981
and I am graduating this year
with my doctorate in History.

00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:16.420
I'm very excited to be one
of this year's recipients

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.460
of the Theron Rockwell Field Prize.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.330
And I wanna go ahead

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:21.990
and begin by congratulating
my fellow winners

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.033
and what I'm sure is fantastic work.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.750
I'd also like to take a moment
to thank my doctoral advisor,

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.990
Gilbert Joseph in the history department,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.320
as well as other members of my committee,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.160
for supporting this research

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.563
before, during, and after
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:43.570
Lastly, I wanna begin by
also thanking my family,

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.380
who's with me here today,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.440
for supporting me ever since
I sat foot on Yale campus

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.513
the first year of my graduate
school program in 2014.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.960
Couldn't have done it without any of you.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:55.793
So thank you.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.137
The title of my dissertation is,

00:00:59.137 --> 00:01:03.690
"From Secret War to Cold
War: Race, Catholicism,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.610
and the Un-making of
Counterrevolutionary Mexico,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.977
1917 to 1946."

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.550
Essentially, my dissertation explores

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.220
a counterrevolution religious movement

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.911
that tried to undo the consequences

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:22.713
of Mexico's seemingly
progressive 1910 Revolution.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.700
Some of the legacies of
this revolution included:

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.410
separation between church and state

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.450
manifested in anti-clerical policies,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.160
the mandating of secular
and socialist education

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.330
for all Mexican youth,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.290
the nationalization of the economy,

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.630
and greater rights for women
and indigenous workers,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.198
etcetera.

00:01:44.198 --> 00:01:48.240
I study the movements that
opposed all of those measures,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.410
as spearheaded by upper
class, middle and upper class,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.010
Catholic women and youth groups.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.870
Now, my dissertation traces
a very interesting evolution

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.513
of political ideologies.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.733
Beginning in 1917,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.740
I began to talk about this
movement as one that has espoused

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.060
and adopted predominantly
democratic and liberal ideals

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.063
in its fight against
anti-clerical authoritarianism.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:18.270
However, by the 1930s,

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.150
we begin to see that the same movement

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.230
began to adopt authoritarian
political models

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.713
and even flirt with fascism,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.380
for reasons which I will
explain in just a second.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.610
Finally, by the early 1940s,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.040
we begin to see a convergence
of church and state interest,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.490
and the effective end
of the counterrevolution

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.440
as it integrated itself

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.653
to the Mexican postrevolutionary state.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.380
My dissertation essentially argues

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.510
that the reason for these
transformations was race.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.400
And the reason why this
movement essentially failed

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.233
was racism.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.270
My dissertation demonstrate
that this was a movement

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.880
that was driven and undone

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.453
primarily by perceptions
and notions of whiteness.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.710
And it takes religious discourse

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:09.150
as presented by these organizations

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.470
and analyzes the racialized dimensions,

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.890
sometimes lying in the subtext

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.133
of what was being explicitly conveyed.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.050
Thus, my dissertation phrases,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.550
the evolution of racism
in Mexican society,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.050
just as it traces

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:30.100
the evolution of this
counterrevolutionary movement,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.150
from a secret war that was
fought between church and state

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.200
not just in Mexico, but
also across the Atlantic,

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.410
particularly in countries
like Spain and the US,

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.820
to a Cold War that brought
church and state together

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.040
and their joint interest

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.003
to suppress working class
indigenous mobilization.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.590
I look forward to talking
more about this work

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.170
throughout today,
tomorrow, and even after.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:56.500
Feel free to follow me
on Twitter actually,

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.503
at RAlvarez-Pi.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.550
I'd like to take a moment
to thank the committee again

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.690
for rewarding my work
with this recognition.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.230
And I'd also like to take a moment

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.173
to congratulate the members
of the class of 2022.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.640
Congratulations.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.610
- Hello, I'm Anna Duensing,

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.090
graduating with a PhD

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:20.050
from the department of History
and African-American studies.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.540
I'm a historian of the United
States in the modern world,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.870
specializing in African-American history

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.630
black internationalism,

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.000
in the evolving politics
of white supremacy

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.040
in the United States
across the 20th century.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.360
And I'm a 2022 winner of the Porter Prize

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.867
for my dissertation,

00:04:36.867 --> 00:04:40.380
"Fascists without Labels:
Jim Crow, Civil Rights,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.540
and the Making of a Black
Antifascist Tradition,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.897
1933 to 1977."

00:04:46.897 --> 00:04:48.210
"Fascist Without Labels"

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.330
chronicles a diffuse network
of black radical thinkers,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.070
activists, and their grassroots
multiracial coalitions,

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:56.820
as they organized to sound alarm

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.940
over homegrown fascism
in the United States,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.100
initially during the age of Jim Crow.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.630
In addition to identifying and
combating white supremacists

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.740
who actively worked to
merge fascist ideologies,

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.510
practices, and aesthetics

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.340
with distinctly American
political traditions,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.140
these figures confronted
what they identified

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:21.470
as undeniable parallels,
echoes, and overlaps

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.400
between global fascism and
the normal daily-functioning

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.070
of politics in the United States.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.720
My work illuminates the richness

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.720
of the black antifascist
tradition of the 1930s and 1940s.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.600
Yet my research is ultimately focused

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.130
on tracking the way
these figures mobilized

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.810
in the post-war period
against fascist afterlife

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.840
possessing segregationist,
and their supporters,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.380
and those many lawmakers, state officials,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.880
and everyday white people
who remained deeply committed

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.210
to a politics of white supremacy
and to anti-black violence,

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:58.750
even after Jim crow's ostensible demise

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.433
with the civil rights
triumphs of the 1960s.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.490
My dissertation offers a
history of black radicalism

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:10.150
and modern conservatism in
conversation and in contestation.

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.310
It tells a story of white
supremacy, racial nationalism,

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.780
vigilantism, and state
violence in the United States,

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.410
while elevating a globally-minded
black freedom struggle

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.080
in which black radical
thinkers, activists,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:26.290
civil rights leaders, and everyday people

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.300
vigilantly analyzed and underscored

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.670
the practices and processes

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.180
that kept fascism alive in this country.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:37.890
Anti-fascism, I argue,
was essential element

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.330
of black activist struggles
for justice and liberation

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.430
across the 20th century.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:44.940
And it is essential history

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.490
for understanding and
navigating our present moment.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.280
Thanks very much for this award

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.623
and congratulations to
everyone graduating this year.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.410
- Hi, I'm Jill Tan, a
third year PhD candidate

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:58.690
in anthropology.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.670
I study death and dying in Singapore,

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.960
and work with a funeral profession.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.180
As a writer, artist, and researcher,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.940
I'm committed to collaborative practice

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.620
and multimodal exploration

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.310
through games, performance, and poetics.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:14.440
I'm a 2022 winner of the Field Prize.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.297
My prize submission is entitled,

00:07:16.297 --> 00:07:20.610
"Notes on the bicentennial
of a f/l/ound/er/ing (2019)"

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.060
This multimedia work of hybrid poetry

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.840
which is video, sculpture,
and performance elements,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.100
grew out of my poem
essay of the same title

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.900
on British imperialism in Singapore.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.083
Through this work,

00:07:33.083 --> 00:07:35.700
I explore how my
subjectivity as a Singaporean

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.692
has resulted in an entrench to reap

00:07:37.692 --> 00:07:39.780
misrecognition of colonial violence

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.773
as a birthmark rather than a scab.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.790
As a Singaporean who has
been taught not to notice

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.470
how the enduring violence

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:50.040
of imperial corporate power
structures my way in the world,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.530
I began making this work
to reflect upon 2019

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:56.620
as the bicentennial of the
British colonization of Singapore

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.560
in 1819.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.260
This work takes several forms;

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:04.430
its origin as poetic text,
voiced as part of a soundscape,

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.670
and as a visually and materially
mediated archival object

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.720
where in paper pulp is molded

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.840
into sculpture, on film,
and in performance.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:14.673
In my work,

00:08:14.673 --> 00:08:17.550
I explore how as a method
and expression of inquiry,

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.120
the poetic holds within
itself a built-in resistance

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:21.770
to foreclosure.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.260
I find that the poetic
is a bespoke contraption

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:25.670
for mapping out gestures,

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:28.520
which also embody the
qualities of research design

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:30.360
that I seek in my ethnographic practice

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.070
as an anthropologist.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.211
The poetic moves, and is moved

00:08:34.211 --> 00:08:36.873
by the ethnographic object in motion.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.070
Following its charge

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.590
can be a way of writing
and doing ethnography

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:43.611
that instantiates and creates.

00:08:43.611 --> 00:08:46.170
It sits to flow and force.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.350
In making this work, the
poetic lent the dual modalities

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.240
of accretion and attrition,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.970
and a visual rather than textual literacy,

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.230
using not only reductions and illusions,

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:58.880
but cleavage and sequence to build my case

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.290
of a lost consciousness.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.240
As I moved through building
by way of unlearning,

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:07.340
I thought of this hybrid poem
not only as a simulacrum,

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:10.290
of archival form and formulation,

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.640
but as an entry in itself.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.680
I not only suggest the text
status as archival object

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.640
through formal mimesis
of a historical document,

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.150
but forge an archival encounter within it,

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:22.940
by excerpting and then deconstructing

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.360
my own writings from 2009

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:28.490
on Singapore's national
historical narratives.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.610
Poetics can serve as a pushback
against the predisposition

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:35.370
towards classification of
the post-colonial archive,

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.880
that narratives almost always feed into

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.780
without deserting the literary entirely.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.810
Without as much stock placed
in arch and teleology,

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:47.970
the poetic, as a contraption
for a contingent moving object,

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.900
destabilizes the archive upon entry.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:52.207
Thank you.

00:09:52.207 --> 00:09:54.957
(logo whooshing)

